export default JSON.parse("{\"2.9.1\":{\"unit\":\"2.9\",\"courseId\":\"2.9.1\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"It's 15:00 in Bucharest on a Wednesday in July. Which of these are open right now: US stocks (regular session), London stocks, spot FX, crypto?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"All four\",\"feedback\":\"Not US stocks yet. 15:00 in Bucharest is 08:00 in New York; the regular session typically opens at 09:30 there, 16:30 for you.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"London stocks, spot FX and crypto\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Spot FX and crypto only\",\"feedback\":\"London is open too: typically 08:00–16:30 London time, which is 10:00–18:30 in Bucharest.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"US stocks, spot FX and crypto\",\"feedback\":\"It's only 08:00 in New York, so the regular session hasn't started. London has: it runs 10:00–18:30 in Bucharest.\"}],\"answer\":\"b\"},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. At 15:00 in Bucharest it's 08:00 in New York: US stocks open at 16:30 your time. London trades 10:00–18:30; FX and crypto never closed.\",\"concept\":\"market-hours\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"One main session each\",\"text\":\"Every market has a main [[trading-session|trading session]]. That's when most orders meet, [[spread|spreads]] are tightest and [[liquidity]] is deepest. Stock exchanges open and close each day; spot [[forex]] trades around the clock on weekdays; [[cryptocurrency|crypto]] never stops. The hours in this lesson are typical: check each exchange's own site, because holidays and half-days change them.\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"A July weekday, in Bucharest\",\"text\":\"Each bar is one market's hours on a July weekday. Read the vertical axis as the hour of the day in Bucharest.\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"chart\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":10,\"h\":18.5,\"l\":10,\"c\":18.5},{\"o\":11,\"h\":16.5,\"l\":11,\"c\":16.5},{\"o\":16.5,\"h\":23,\"l\":16.5,\"c\":23},{\"o\":0,\"h\":24,\"l\":0,\"c\":24},{\"o\":0,\"h\":24,\"l\":0,\"c\":24}],\"range\":[0,24],\"height\":260,\"describe\":\"Five vertical bars show trading hours in Bucharest time on a July weekday, with the hour of the day on the vertical axis. From the left: London stocks 10:00 to 18:30, US pre-market 11:00 to 16:30, US regular session 16:30 to 23:00, spot FX all day, crypto all day.\"},\"steps\":[{\"text\":\"From the left: London stocks, typically 10:00–18:30 in Bucharest.\"},{\"text\":\"US stocks: pre-market from 11:00 at many brokers, then the regular session, 16:30–23:00.\"},{\"text\":\"Spot FX and crypto: open all day on a weekday.\"},{\"text\":\"At 15:00, London, FX and crypto are open. US stocks are still in pre-market.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":15,\"role\":\"concept\",\"label\":\"15:00\",\"chip\":false}]}]},\"concept\":\"market-hours\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Hours by market\",\"prompt\":\"Match each market to its typical hours. Exact hours vary by exchange and venue, so check them before you rely on them.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"US stocks, regular session\",\"right\":\"09:30–16:00 New York (16:30–23:00 in Bucharest)\"},{\"left\":\"London Stock Exchange\",\"right\":\"08:00–16:30 London (10:00–18:30 in Bucharest)\"},{\"left\":\"Spot FX\",\"right\":\"Sunday 17:00 to Friday 17:00 New York\",\"wrong\":\"FX trades around the clock on weekdays, from Sunday evening in New York to Friday's 17:00 close.\"},{\"left\":\"US index futures\",\"right\":\"Sunday 18:00 to Friday 17:00 New York, with a one-hour daily break\",\"wrong\":\"Futures trade nearly around the clock too, but pause for an hour each day, 17:00–18:00 New York.\"},{\"left\":\"Crypto\",\"right\":\"Around the clock, every day\"}],\"columns\":[\"Market\",\"Typical hours\"],\"concept\":\"market-hours\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"The week the clocks disagree\",\"prompt\":\"Between 25 October and 1 November 2026, what time does the US stock market open in Bucharest?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"16:30, as usual\",\"feedback\":\"16:30 holds most of the year. That week Europe has left summer time but the US hasn't, so New York is 6 hours behind, not 7.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"15:30\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"17:30\",\"feedback\":\"The gap shrinks rather than grows: Europe moves its clocks back first, so New York is 6 hours behind Bucharest, not 8.\"}],\"answer\":\"b\"},\"reveal\":\"15:30. The EU leaves [[daylight-saving-time|daylight saving time]] on the last Sunday of October (25 October 2026), the US on the first Sunday of November (1 November). In spring the US switches first, on the second Sunday of March: from 8 to 29 March 2026, US events also come an hour early for you.\",\"concept\":\"market-hours\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"Your platform's clock may not be yours. Many FX platforms run on [[server-time|server time]], commonly GMT+2 or GMT+3, so the daily candle closes at 17:00 New York time. Before you plan around a time, check which clock your chart uses.\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Open is not the same as liquid\",\"text\":\"Spot FX trades all day on weekdays, but not evenly. Most orders arrive while London and New York are open. After New York closes and before Asia gets busy, [[spread|spreads]] tend to widen and prices move in jumpy steps. Crypto runs 24/7, yet its weekends are often quieter too. A 24-hour market has busy hours and empty ones.\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Convert a misaligned Friday\",\"prompt\":\"Friday 30 October 2026: Europe is on winter time, the US still on summer time. Convert each event to Bucharest time.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"US stock open, 09:30 New York\",\"right\":\"15:30\",\"wrong\":\"16:30 is the usual time. This week New York is 6 hours behind Bucharest, not 7.\"},{\"left\":\"London open, 08:00 London\",\"right\":\"10:00\",\"wrong\":\"London is always 2 hours behind Bucharest: both change their clocks on the same dates.\"},{\"left\":\"An NFP-style US jobs report, 08:30 New York\",\"right\":\"14:30\",\"wrong\":\"Add 6 hours this week, not 7: 08:30 becomes 14:30.\"},{\"left\":\"An ECB-style rate decision, 14:15 Frankfurt\",\"right\":\"15:15\",\"wrong\":\"Frankfurt is 1 hour behind Bucharest all year: 14:15 becomes 15:15.\"},{\"left\":\"FX rollover, 17:00 New York\",\"right\":\"23:00\",\"wrong\":\"Usually midnight in Bucharest. This week it's 23:00, 6 hours after 17:00.\"}],\"columns\":[\"Event, local time\",\"Bucharest\"],\"correct\":\"All five. European times keep their usual gap to Bucharest all year; only the New York times shift, by one hour, in these weeks.\",\"concept\":\"market-hours\"}],\"check\":[\"q-2.9.1-1\",\"q-2.9.1-2\",\"q-2.9.1-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Main sessions in Bucharest, typically: London 10:00–18:30, US stocks 16:30–23:00; FX and crypto run longer.\",\"When US and EU clocks change on different dates, New York events come an hour earlier for you.\",\"Open isn't liquid: a 24-hour market is busiest when London and New York trade.\"]}},\"2.9.2\":{\"unit\":\"2.9\",\"courseId\":\"2.9.2\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"On a major FX pair, which 4-hour window typically moves the most: the Asian session, the London morning, the London–New York overlap, or the New York afternoon?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The Asian session\",\"feedback\":\"Tokyo is a big centre, but Asian hours are usually the quietest for most pairs. Ranges stay small.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The London morning\",\"feedback\":\"London's open is busy, and London is the largest FX centre. But the range grows further once New York joins.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The London–New York overlap\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The New York afternoon\",\"feedback\":\"Once London closes, activity usually fades. The afternoon is calmer than the overlap before it.\"}],\"answer\":\"c\"},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. The overlap, when the two biggest centres trade together: typically 15:00–19:00 in Bucharest, an hour earlier in the weeks when US and EU clocks disagree.\",\"concept\":\"fx-sessions\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Four sessions, one overlap\",\"text\":\"FX follows the sun through four loose sessions. In New York time, roughly: Sydney 17:00–02:00, Tokyo 19:00–04:00, London 03:00–12:00, New York 08:00–17:00. London and New York overlap from 08:00 to 12:00 New York time, which is typically 15:00–19:00 in Bucharest: the [[session-overlap|session overlap]].\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"A day's rhythm in FX\",\"text\":\"A synthetic profile of a major pair: the average range of each 4-hour window, in Bucharest time. The shape follows studies of real FX trading; the numbers are illustrative.\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"bars\",\"bars\":[{\"label\":\"23:00–03:00\",\"value\":12,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"03:00–07:00\",\"value\":18,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"07:00–11:00\",\"value\":24,\"role\":\"accent\"},{\"label\":\"11:00–15:00\",\"value\":30,\"role\":\"accent\"},{\"label\":\"15:00–19:00\",\"value\":42,\"role\":\"up\"},{\"label\":\"19:00–23:00\",\"value\":21,\"role\":\"neutral\"}],\"unit\":\" pips\",\"steps\":[\"Asian hours, 23:00–07:00: Tokyo trades, but ranges stay small.\",\"London opens at 10:00 Bucharest time, and the range grows.\",\"15:00–19:00: London and New York together, the peak. Then the afternoon fades.\"]},\"concept\":\"fx-sessions\"},{\"kind\":\"hotspot\",\"title\":\"Find the overlap\",\"prompt\":\"One synthetic day of a major pair in six 4-hour candles, the first starting at 23:00 Bucharest time. Prices are scaled so 0.01 is one pip. Tap the candle that covers the London–New York overlap.\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":100,\"h\":100.07,\"l\":99.96,\"c\":100.03},{\"o\":100.03,\"h\":100.12,\"l\":99.96,\"c\":100.09},{\"o\":100.09,\"h\":100.24,\"l\":99.99,\"c\":100.2},{\"o\":100.2,\"h\":100.38,\"l\":100.09,\"c\":100.31},{\"o\":100.31,\"h\":100.52,\"l\":100.08,\"c\":100.12},{\"o\":100.12,\"h\":100.22,\"l\":100.03,\"c\":100.18}],\"describe\":\"Six 4-hour candles of a synthetic FX day, starting at 23:00 Bucharest time. Their ranges are 11, 16, 25, 29, 44 and 19 pips; the fifth, 15:00 to 19:00, is the widest.\"},\"zones\":[{\"id\":\"z0\",\"label\":\"23:00–03:00\",\"index\":0,\"feedback\":\"That's late New York and Sydney hours, the quietest of the day.\"},{\"id\":\"z1\",\"label\":\"03:00–07:00\",\"index\":1,\"feedback\":\"Tokyo hours. London hasn't opened yet.\"},{\"id\":\"z2\",\"label\":\"07:00–11:00\",\"index\":2,\"feedback\":\"Tokyo's end and London's open at 10:00. New York is still asleep.\"},{\"id\":\"z3\",\"label\":\"11:00–15:00\",\"index\":3,\"feedback\":\"London alone. New York joins at 15:00 Bucharest time.\"},{\"id\":\"z4\",\"label\":\"15:00–19:00\",\"index\":4},{\"id\":\"z5\",\"label\":\"19:00–23:00\",\"index\":5,\"feedback\":\"London has closed by then. This is the New York afternoon.\"}],\"answer\":\"z4\",\"correct\":\"Right: 15:00–19:00 in Bucharest is 08:00–12:00 in New York, when London and New York are both open. It's also the day's widest candle: 44 pips.\",\"concept\":\"fx-sessions\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Your clock, their sessions\",\"prompt\":\"Most of the year, in Bucharest time. Match each window to what happens in FX.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"About 02:00–11:00\",\"right\":\"Tokyo session: yen pairs wake up\",\"wrong\":\"Tokyo's session is roughly 19:00–04:00 New York time: 02:00–11:00 in Bucharest.\"},{\"left\":\"10:00\",\"right\":\"London opens: euro and pound pairs pick up\",\"wrong\":\"London's 08:00 is 10:00 in Bucharest, all year.\"},{\"left\":\"15:00–19:00\",\"right\":\"London and New York overlap: the busiest hours\"},{\"left\":\"00:00\",\"right\":\"New York closes and FX rolls over: a thin market\",\"wrong\":\"17:00 in New York, the end of the FX day, is midnight in Bucharest most of the year.\"}],\"columns\":[\"Bucharest time\",\"What happens\"],\"correct\":\"All four. A pair tends to be busier when the centres of its own currencies are open: yen pairs in Tokyo hours, euro pairs in London and the overlap.\",\"concept\":\"fx-sessions\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Same target, different bill\",\"lines\":[\"A 10-pip target on a major pair.\",\"In the overlap the spread is 0.8 pips: 0.8 ÷ 10 = 8% of the target.\",\"At 01:00 the spread is 1.6 pips. Share of the target = 1.6 ÷ 10 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":16,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"%\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":0.16,\"text\":\"That's the ratio. As a percentage: 16%.\"},{\"value\":6.25,\"text\":\"That's 10 ÷ 1.6, upside down. Divide the spread by the target.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. 16%: the night trade pays twice the overlap's share before price moves.\"},\"after\":\"Spreads are tight when many traders compete and wide when few do. At night you pay more and the range is smaller: a double [[cost-drag|cost drag]].\",\"concept\":\"fx-sessions\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"'London sets the day's high or low' and 'killzones' are folklore. The busy windows around the opens bring more movement, not a known direction. No tested evidence shows that a session's hour predicts which way price goes (grade D). Use the hours for movement per unit of cost; nothing more.\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Movement per pip paid\",\"lines\":[\"A breakout rule, traded in one 4-hour window. Illustrative averages, in pips:\",\"Asian, 03:00–07:00: range 18, spread 1.5 → 12 pips of range per pip of spread.\",\"London morning, 11:00–15:00: range 30, spread 1.0 → 30.\",\"New York afternoon, 19:00–23:00: range 21, spread 1.2 → 17.5.\",\"Overlap, 15:00–19:00: range 42, spread 0.8 → ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":52.5,\"tolerance\":0.05,\"feedback\":[{\"value\":33.6,\"text\":\"That's 42 × 0.8. Divide the range by the spread: 42 ÷ 0.8.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. 52.5 pips of range for every pip of spread: the best of the four.\"},\"after\":\"Pick the window by movement per unit of cost, from your own data. The overlap usually wins that test, not because a session 'sets the high'.\",\"concept\":\"fx-sessions\"}],\"check\":[\"q-2.9.2-1\",\"q-2.9.2-2\",\"q-2.9.2-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Sydney, Tokyo, London, New York; the London–New York overlap is typically 15:00–19:00 in Bucharest.\",\"More traders mean more movement and tighter spreads; quiet hours cost more for less.\",\"Choose hours by range per unit of spread, not by session folklore or killzones.\"]}},\"2.9.3\":{\"unit\":\"2.9\",\"courseId\":\"2.9.3\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"Your stop is 0.40 below entry. In the first 30 minutes LABX's 5-minute ATR is 0.60; at midday it is 0.20. When is the stop inside the noise?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"At the open\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"At midday\",\"feedback\":\"At midday a typical 5-minute candle moves 0.20, half the stop. Noise alone rarely reaches it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Both\",\"feedback\":\"At midday the stop is twice the 5-minute ATR. Only at the open is it smaller than one candle's normal move.\"}],\"answer\":\"a\"},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. At the open one normal 5-minute candle moves 0.60, more than the 0.40 stop. At midday the same stop is two candles' worth.\",\"concept\":\"intraday-cycle\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"A U-shaped day\",\"text\":\"In stock markets, volume and [[volatility]] are typically highest just after the open and before the close, and lowest around midday: a U shape. In FX the U shows up inside each session. In this lesson LABX follows the same rhythm, so a stop that fits midday can sit inside the noise at the open.\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"LABX's day in 30-minute windows\",\"text\":\"LABX's synthetic 5-minute [[atr|ATR]] in each 30-minute window of a 09:30–16:00 session, shaped like real stock data.\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"bars\",\"bars\":[{\"label\":\"09:30\",\"value\":0.6,\"role\":\"accent\"},{\"label\":\"10:00\",\"value\":0.48,\"role\":\"accent\"},{\"label\":\"10:30\",\"value\":0.38,\"role\":\"accent\"},{\"label\":\"11:00\",\"value\":0.31,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"11:30\",\"value\":0.26,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"12:00\",\"value\":0.22,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"12:30\",\"value\":0.2,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"13:00\",\"value\":0.2,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"13:30\",\"value\":0.22,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"14:00\",\"value\":0.26,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"14:30\",\"value\":0.31,\"role\":\"neutral\"},{\"label\":\"15:00\",\"value\":0.37,\"role\":\"accent\"},{\"label\":\"15:30\",\"value\":0.45,\"role\":\"accent\"}],\"steps\":[\"The open: 0.60 in the first 30 minutes, then falling fast.\",\"Midday: as low as 0.20, a third of the open.\",\"Into the close, activity returns: 0.45 in the last 30 minutes.\"]},\"concept\":\"intraday-cycle\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Auctions at both ends\",\"text\":\"Many exchanges open and close with an auction. In the [[opening-auction|opening auction]] and the [[closing-auction|closing auction]], orders collected over a few minutes are crossed at a single price. The closing price is the official close that index funds track, so a lot of volume waits for it. That's one reason the end of the day is busy.\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Midday: a tight stop and a cap\",\"lines\":[\"Rule: stop = 2 × the window's ATR. Midday ATR is 0.20, so the stop is 0.40: 99.60 for an entry at 100.00.\",\"By risk, 1% (100.00) would allow 200 units: 99.96 lost at the stop.\",\"Without leverage, value plus the entry fee must fit in 10,000.00: 99 units at most. They risk 49.49, about 0.5%.\",\"Costs in R: 0.20 per unit ÷ 0.40 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":0.5,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"R\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":2,\"text\":\"That's 0.40 ÷ 0.20, upside down. Divide the cost by the stop distance.\"},{\"value\":0.17,\"text\":\"That's the open: 0.20 ÷ 1.20. At midday the stop is 0.40.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. 0.50R: half of every R goes to costs.\"},\"after\":\"At the open the stop is 1.20 and costs are 0.20 ÷ 1.20 = 0.17R. Quiet hours shrink the stop, so the same costs take a bigger slice of each R.\",\"concept\":\"costs-in-r\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Where the stop-outs land\",\"prompt\":\"A trader keeps a fixed 0.40 stop all day, whatever the hour. In which window will most of the stop-outs land?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The first 30 minutes\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Midday\",\"feedback\":\"At midday 0.40 is two ATRs, and noise rarely reaches it. The trouble is where one candle's normal move is bigger than the stop.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Evenly through the day\",\"feedback\":\"The stop is the same all day, but the noise isn't: 0.60 per candle at the open, 0.20 at midday.\"}],\"answer\":\"a\"},\"reveal\":\"The first 30 minutes. There 0.40 is two thirds of one candle's normal move; near the close, about nine tenths; at midday, two full ATRs. Log the time of every stop-out: a cluster at the open means the stop, not the setup, is the problem.\",\"concept\":\"intraday-cycle\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"The cap is a brake, not a target. At midday the right stop allows only 0.5% risk, because 99 units is all 10,000.00 can buy. Don't 'fill' the missing half with [[leverage]]: it adds exposure, not edge. Margin and leverage come in 2.14.6.\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"position-size\",\"goal\":\"At the open, LABX's 5-minute ATR is 0.60. Set the stop at 2 × ATR below the 100.00 entry, then type the most units that lose at most 100.00 there, costs included.\",\"start\":{\"account\":10000,\"riskPct\":1,\"entry\":100,\"stop\":99.6,\"units\":99},\"locked\":[\"account\",\"riskPct\",\"entry\"],\"hidden\":[\"simpleQty\",\"qty\"],\"success\":{\"all\":[{\"field\":\"stop\",\"eq\":98.8},{\"field\":\"units\",\"eq\":76}]},\"feedback\":[{\"when\":{\"field\":\"stop\",\"gt\":98.8},\"text\":\"A stop at {stop:2} is less than 2 × 0.60 below the entry. At the open it sits inside the noise.\"},{\"when\":{\"field\":\"stop\",\"lt\":98.8},\"text\":\"That's wider than the rule: 2 × 0.60 = 1.20, so the stop goes at 98.80.\"},{\"when\":{\"field\":\"units\",\"eq\":77},\"text\":\"77 units lose 100.06 at 98.80 with both commissions, just over 100.00.\"},{\"when\":{\"field\":\"units\",\"gt\":77},\"text\":\"{units} units would lose {lossAtStop:2} at the stop, over 100.00.\"},{\"when\":{\"field\":\"units\",\"lt\":76},\"text\":\"{units} units fit, losing {lossAtStop:2}. One more would still fit: find the most that does.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. 76 units lose 98.76 at 98.80, within 1%, and costs are 0.20 ÷ 1.20 = 0.17R. Near the close, ATR 0.45 gives a 0.90 stop, and the 99-unit cap binds again.\",\"hints\":[\"2 × 0.60 = 1.20, so the stop goes at 100.00 − 1.20.\",\"Each unit loses 1.20 at the stop, plus about 0.10 in commissions.\",\"100.00 ÷ 1.2994 ≈ 76.96. Round down.\"],\"showMe\":{\"stop\":98.8,\"units\":76},\"concept\":\"intraday-cycle\"},\"check\":[\"q-2.9.3-1\",\"q-2.9.3-2\",\"q-2.9.3-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Volume and volatility form a U: high at the open and close, low at midday.\",\"Set the stop from the hour's ATR: 2 × 0.60 = 1.20 at the open, 0.40 at midday.\",\"Quiet hours shrink stops, so costs take more of each R: 0.50R at midday, 0.17R at the open.\"]}},\"2.9.4\":{\"unit\":\"2.9\",\"courseId\":\"2.9.4\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"In a replication, the opening range breakout made about +0.13R per trade before costs. A round trip costs about 0.10R, and stop orders at the open slip. What's left per trade?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"slider\",\"min\":-0.2,\"max\":0.2,\"step\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\",\"start\":0.13,\"answer\":0,\"tolerance\":0.03,\"feedback\":[{\"from\":0.1,\"to\":0.2,\"text\":\"That's about the gross edge. Costs come out of every trade: 0.13 − 0.10 leaves +0.03R before slippage.\"},{\"from\":0.04,\"to\":0.09,\"text\":\"Costs alone leave +0.03R, and slippage on stop orders at the open takes more.\"},{\"from\":-0.2,\"to\":-0.04,\"text\":\"Bad fills can do that. Costs alone leave +0.03R; with slippage the replication found nothing distinguishable from zero.\"}]},\"compare\":\"You estimated {yours}. 0.13 − 0.10 leaves +0.03R before slippage. With slippage, the replication found no result distinguishable from zero.\",\"concept\":\"opening-range-breakout\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"The rules\",\"text\":\"The [[opening-range|opening range]] is the high and low of the session's first N minutes, with N fixed in advance: 5, 15, 30 or 60. An [[orb|ORB]] places a buy [[stop-order|stop order]] just above the high, or a sell stop just below the low. The stop-loss sits at the other edge; the exit is a target or the close.\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"A 15-minute opening range\",\"text\":\"LABX on 5-minute candles, from the open.\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"chart\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":100,\"h\":100.4,\"l\":99.85,\"c\":100.2},{\"o\":100.2,\"h\":100.3,\"l\":99.6,\"c\":99.75},{\"o\":99.75,\"h\":100.1,\"l\":99.7,\"c\":100.05},{\"o\":100.05,\"h\":100.25,\"l\":99.9,\"c\":100.15},{\"o\":100.15,\"h\":100.35,\"l\":100.05,\"c\":100.3},{\"o\":100.3,\"h\":100.62,\"l\":100.25,\"c\":100.58},{\"o\":100.58,\"h\":100.85,\"l\":100.5,\"c\":100.8},{\"o\":100.8,\"h\":101.05,\"l\":100.7,\"c\":100.95},{\"o\":100.95,\"h\":101.2,\"l\":100.85,\"c\":101.1},{\"o\":101.1,\"h\":101.25,\"l\":100.95,\"c\":101.05}],\"visible\":3},\"steps\":[{\"text\":\"LABX opens at 100.00. The first three 5-minute candles make the 15-minute range.\",\"visible\":3},{\"text\":\"Opening range: 99.60 to 100.40, 0.80 tall.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"zone\",\"from\":99.6,\"to\":100.4,\"role\":\"concept\",\"label\":\"Opening range\"}]},{\"text\":\"Daily ATR(14) is 2.00, so the range is 40% of a normal day: not huge.\"},{\"text\":\"A buy stop at 100.45 fills on the break; the stop-loss sits at 99.55.\",\"visible\":10,\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":100.45,\"role\":\"entry\",\"label\":\"Buy stop\"},{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":99.55,\"role\":\"stop\",\"label\":\"Stop\"}]}]},\"concept\":\"opening-range-breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Size it, then price the costs\",\"lines\":[\"Entry 100.45, stop 99.55: 0.90 away.\",\"At 1%, 99 units lose 99.01 at the stop; 100 would lose 100.01. Value: 9,944.55.\",\"Costs in R: 0.20 ÷ 0.90 = 0.22R.\",\"A tighter variant puts the stop at a tenth of the daily ATR: 0.20. Costs in R = 0.20 ÷ 0.20 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":1,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":0.1,\"text\":\"That's 0.20 ÷ 2.00, the ATR. Divide by the stop distance, 0.20.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. 1.0R: a full R in costs on every trade.\"},\"after\":\"With LABX's costs the tight variant can't survive: each trade pays its whole risk again in costs. The range-edge stop keeps costs at 0.22R, still more than the +0.13R gross edge in the hook.\",\"concept\":\"costs-in-r\"},{\"kind\":\"reveal\",\"title\":\"A quiet day\",\"prompt\":\"No news today and light volume. Price has just broken above the opening range, and the buy stop at 100.40 filled. What happens next?\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":100,\"h\":100.3,\"l\":99.8,\"c\":100.1},{\"o\":100.1,\"h\":100.35,\"l\":99.75,\"c\":99.9},{\"o\":99.9,\"h\":100.2,\"l\":99.7,\"c\":100.05},{\"o\":100.05,\"h\":100.3,\"l\":99.95,\"c\":100.25},{\"o\":100.25,\"h\":100.48,\"l\":100.15,\"c\":100.42},{\"o\":100.42,\"h\":100.46,\"l\":100.05,\"c\":100.1},{\"o\":100.1,\"h\":100.15,\"l\":99.8,\"c\":99.85},{\"o\":99.85,\"h\":99.9,\"l\":99.62,\"c\":99.65},{\"o\":99.65,\"h\":99.95,\"l\":99.58,\"c\":99.9},{\"o\":99.9,\"h\":100.2,\"l\":99.85,\"c\":100.15},{\"o\":100.15,\"h\":100.25,\"l\":100,\"c\":100.05}],\"visible\":5,\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"zone\",\"from\":99.7,\"to\":100.35,\"role\":\"concept\",\"label\":\"Opening range\"},{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":100.4,\"role\":\"entry\",\"label\":\"Buy stop\"},{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":99.65,\"role\":\"stop\",\"label\":\"Stop\"}]},\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It runs higher into the close\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It falls back into the range\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It goes nowhere for the rest of the day\"}],\"answer\":\"b\",\"after\":\"It fell back, hit the long's stop under the range, then broke the low and failed again: two losers in one quiet day. On a trend day the first break runs; on a news day price can jump past your order. Range days are where ORB bleeds.\",\"concept\":\"opening-range-breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Filters and what they remove\",\"prompt\":\"Each filter removes a kind of day on which ORB tends to fail. Match them.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Only days 'in play': a catalyst and unusually high relative volume\",\"right\":\"Quiet days, where breaks tend to fail\"},{\"left\":\"Skip if the opening range is already most of a daily ATR\",\"right\":\"Stops too wide, with much of the day's move spent\"},{\"left\":\"Stand aside around a scheduled release\",\"right\":\"Stop orders filled far past their price\"},{\"left\":\"Fix N before the session starts\",\"right\":\"Choosing the range length after seeing the chart\"}],\"columns\":[\"Filter\",\"Removes\"],\"concept\":\"opening-range-breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Evidence: grade C\",\"text\":\"A working paper (Zarattini, Barbon and Aziz, 2024) reported large net returns from ORB on US 'stocks in play', with no peer review or out-of-sample test. An independent replication on five index CFDs found a gross edge, about +0.13R per trade on one. After spread and slippage, none was distinguishable from zero; four were negative.\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"The sign flips\",\"lines\":[\"One configuration on LABX: 15-minute range, in-play days only, range-edge stop 0.90 away.\",\"Suppose the gross result, at zero cost, is +0.13R per trade.\",\"Retail round trip: 0.20 ÷ 0.90 = 0.22R. Slippage on the entry: 0.05 ÷ 0.90 = 0.06R.\",\"Net per trade at retail cost = 0.13 − 0.22 − 0.06 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":-0.15,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"R\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":0.13,\"text\":\"That's the gross edge, before any costs.\"},{\"value\":-0.09,\"text\":\"You left out the slippage: another 0.06R.\"},{\"value\":0.15,\"text\":\"Check the sign: the costs are bigger than the edge.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. −0.15R per trade.\"},\"after\":\"+0.13R at zero cost, −0.15R at retail cost: the sign flips. Every intraday edge has to be judged after costs, and at the open costs are at their highest.\",\"concept\":\"opening-range-breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"The pattern is real. Whether it pays depends on costs: the gross edge reported for ORB is about the size of one round trip.\"}],\"check\":[\"q-2.9.4-1\",\"q-2.9.4-2\",\"q-2.9.4-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Opening range: first N minutes' high and low. ORB: stop order beyond one edge, stop at the other.\",\"A +0.13R gross edge turns into −0.15R after LABX's costs and slippage at the open.\",\"Trade it only on in-play days with a modest range; skip quiet days and releases.\"]}},\"2.9.5\":{\"unit\":\"2.9\",\"courseId\":\"2.9.5\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"LABX closes at 100.00 and opens at 101.00 with no news. On a pure random walk with a daily standard deviation of 1.60, how often would price touch 100.00 again that day?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"slider\",\"min\":0,\"max\":100,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"%\",\"start\":20,\"answer\":53,\"tolerance\":5,\"feedback\":[{\"from\":0,\"to\":40,\"text\":\"Higher. A 1.00 gap is well under one day's standard deviation, so chance alone brings price back often.\"},{\"from\":66,\"to\":100,\"text\":\"Lower. Price has to travel 1.00 back with no pull at all; about half the time it does.\"}]},\"compare\":\"You guessed {yours}. On a random walk, about 53%: a gap fills that often with no cause at all. A 2.50 gap is revisited only about 12% of the time.\",\"concept\":\"gap-strategies\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Two strategies and a pass\",\"text\":\"[[gap-and-go|Gap and go]] trades with a large gap that has a catalyst and heavy [[relative-volume|relative volume]]. Gap fade trades against a moderate gap with no news, back toward the previous close: a [[gap-fill|gap fill]]. Pass is the third choice, often the right one. Gap and go is grade C to D, gap fill C: practitioners' rules, little tested evidence.\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"Three gaps\",\"text\":\"Three opening gaps on daily LABX candles, with volume underneath. The names come from the gap types in 2.5.\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"chart\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":100,\"h\":100.8,\"l\":99.4,\"c\":100.3,\"v\":100},{\"o\":100.3,\"h\":100.9,\"l\":99.7,\"c\":99.9,\"v\":95},{\"o\":99.9,\"h\":100.5,\"l\":99.2,\"c\":100.1,\"v\":105},{\"o\":100.1,\"h\":100.7,\"l\":99.6,\"c\":100.2,\"v\":98},{\"o\":100.9,\"h\":101.1,\"l\":100.1,\"c\":100.3,\"v\":102},{\"o\":100.3,\"h\":100.8,\"l\":99.8,\"c\":100.4,\"v\":97},{\"o\":100.4,\"h\":100.9,\"l\":99.9,\"c\":100.6,\"v\":100},{\"o\":103.2,\"h\":104.3,\"l\":102.9,\"c\":104.1,\"v\":320},{\"o\":104.1,\"h\":105,\"l\":103.7,\"c\":104.8,\"v\":180},{\"o\":104.8,\"h\":105.6,\"l\":104.4,\"c\":105.4,\"v\":150},{\"o\":105.4,\"h\":106.1,\"l\":105,\"c\":105.9,\"v\":140},{\"o\":105.9,\"h\":106.7,\"l\":105.5,\"c\":106.5,\"v\":130},{\"o\":106.5,\"h\":107.3,\"l\":106.1,\"c\":107.1,\"v\":135},{\"o\":107.1,\"h\":107.9,\"l\":106.8,\"c\":107.7,\"v\":140},{\"o\":107.7,\"h\":108.6,\"l\":107.4,\"c\":108.4,\"v\":150},{\"o\":108.4,\"h\":109.3,\"l\":108.1,\"c\":109.1,\"v\":160},{\"o\":109.1,\"h\":110,\"l\":108.8,\"c\":109.8,\"v\":175},{\"o\":111.2,\"h\":111.9,\"l\":110.6,\"c\":110.9,\"v\":380},{\"o\":110.9,\"h\":111,\"l\":109.4,\"c\":109.6,\"v\":260},{\"o\":109.6,\"h\":109.9,\"l\":108.3,\"c\":108.5,\"v\":210},{\"o\":108.5,\"h\":108.9,\"l\":107.4,\"c\":107.7,\"v\":190}],\"volume\":true},\"steps\":[{\"text\":\"A small gap up, no news, normal volume: filled the same day.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":4,\"at\":\"open\",\"label\":\"Common gap\",\"role\":\"neutral\"}]},{\"text\":\"A big gap up on news, with three times the volume. It never came back.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":7,\"at\":\"low\",\"label\":\"Breakaway gap\",\"role\":\"up\"}]},{\"text\":\"After a long run, one more gap on huge volume, then a reversal. Only hindsight names it exhaustion.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":17,\"at\":\"high\",\"label\":\"Exhaustion gap\",\"role\":\"down\"}]}]},\"concept\":\"gap-types\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"The random-walk base rate\",\"lines\":[\"Φ(z) is the chance a normal variable lands below z. By the reflection principle, the chance a random walk comes back a distance d within the day is 2 × (1 − Φ(d ÷ σ)).\",\"Gap 1.00, σ 1.60: Φ(0.625) = 0.734, so 2 × 0.266 = 53%.\",\"Gap 2.50: Φ(1.5625) = 0.941. Chance = 2 × (1 − 0.941) = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":11.8,\"tolerance\":0.3,\"unit\":\"%\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":94.1,\"text\":\"That's Φ itself. Take 1 − 0.941, then double it.\"},{\"value\":5.9,\"text\":\"That's one side. The reflection principle doubles it: 2 × 0.059.\"},{\"value\":0.118,\"text\":\"Right ratio; type it as a percentage: 11.8.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. About 12%.\"},\"after\":\"Small gaps get revisited often by chance alone; big ones rarely. The formula assumes smooth movement; on real candles, which move in steps, it comes out slightly lower.\",\"concept\":\"gap-strategies\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Sort the morning's gaps\",\"prompt\":\"Six morning gaps. Sort each by the card: Go, Fade or Pass. Check the catalyst first, then relative volume, then where price sits against last week's range.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Up 4%: earnings beat, relative volume 5×\",\"right\":\"Go\"},{\"left\":\"Up 1%: no news, normal volume, inside last week's range\",\"right\":\"Fade\"},{\"left\":\"Down 3%: guidance cut, relative volume 4×\",\"right\":\"Go\",\"wrong\":\"A catalyst and heavy volume point to continuation, here downward. Fading it means standing in front of real news.\"},{\"left\":\"Down 1.2%: no news, light volume, inside last week's range\",\"right\":\"Fade\"},{\"left\":\"Up 3%: a rumour only, relative volume 1.2×\",\"right\":\"Pass\",\"wrong\":\"No confirmed catalyst and ordinary volume: not a Go. Too big and unexplained for a Fade. Neither setup is there.\"},{\"left\":\"Up 6% after a 10-day run, huge volume, no news\",\"right\":\"Pass\",\"wrong\":\"It might be exhaustion, but that can only be named in hindsight. With no catalyst and no card that fits, pass.\"}],\"columns\":[\"Gap\",\"Decision\"],\"correct\":\"All six. Pass isn't indecision: it's the card saying neither setup is there.\",\"concept\":\"gap-strategies\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"A gap can be a chart artefact\",\"text\":\"On a chart of the main session, the jump from yesterday's close to today's open is a [[gap]]. On a 24-hour chart of the same [[futures]] or [[cfd|CFD]], price traded all night and the gap may vanish. Before you trade a gap, know which session your chart draws.\"},{\"kind\":\"order\",\"title\":\"The gap decision\",\"prompt\":\"Put the steps of the gap decision in order.\",\"items\":[\"Is there a real catalyst?\",\"How high is the relative volume?\",\"Where is price against last week's range?\",\"Check the setup card's thresholds\",\"Decide: Go, Fade or Pass\"],\"explain\":\"The catalyst and the volume say whether the gap has a cause. Position and the card say which trade fits, if any. The decision comes last.\",\"concept\":\"gap-strategies\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"warning\",\"text\":\"'Gaps always fill' is false, and many fills prove nothing. On a [[random-walk|random walk]] a 1.00 gap is revisited about half the time with no edge at all. A gap-fill strategy has to beat that base rate after costs, not just fill often.\"}],\"check\":[\"q-2.9.5-1\",\"q-2.9.5-2\",\"q-2.9.5-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"A catalyst plus heavy relative volume can carry a gap; without them, gaps tend to fade.\",\"Go with news and volume, fade a quiet gap inside last week's range, otherwise pass.\",\"On a random walk, a 1.00 gap with σ 1.60 is revisited 53% of the time.\"]}},\"2.9.6\":{\"unit\":\"2.9\",\"courseId\":\"2.9.6\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"Same gross edge: +0.30R per trade. Day trader: 25 trades a week, stop 0.50, costs 0.40R per trade. Swing trader: 3 trades a week, stop 3.00, costs 0.07R. Who keeps more per week?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The day trader\",\"feedback\":\"Per trade the day trader pays 0.40R against a 0.30R edge: −0.10R each time, 25 times a week.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The swing trader\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The same: the edge is the same\",\"feedback\":\"Same gross edge, different costs. The tight stop makes the day trader's costs bigger than the edge.\"}],\"answer\":\"b\"},\"compare\":\"You picked {yours}. Swing: (0.30 − 0.07) × 3 = +0.69R a week. Day: (0.30 − 0.40) × 25 = −2.50R. More trades multiply a negative edge.\",\"concept\":\"trading-styles\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Three styles, three bills\",\"text\":\"[[day-trading|Day traders]] are flat by the close: no overnight [[gap]], but many trades, tight stops and hours at the screen. [[swing-trading|Swing traders]] hold days to weeks; [[position-trading|position traders]], weeks to months. The shorter the hold, the more often you pay costs, and the bigger the share of each R they take. Longer holds trade costs for gap risk.\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"The weekly bill in R\",\"lines\":[\"Costs in R per trade = round-trip cost ÷ stop distance.\",\"The day plan: 0.20 ÷ 0.50 = 0.40R a trade; 25 trades = 10.00R a week.\",\"Your plan: 10 trades a week, stop 1.00 on LABX near 100. Weekly costs = 10 × (0.20 ÷ 1.00) = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":2,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":0.2,\"text\":\"That's one trade. Multiply by the 10 trades a week.\"},{\"value\":20,\"text\":\"Costs per trade are 0.20 ÷ 1.00 = 0.20R, not 2R. Then times 10.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. 2.00R a week.\"},\"after\":\"2.00R a week is about 100R over 50 weeks, paid before your first mistake. Fewer trades or wider stops cut the bill; tighter stops or more trades raise it.\",\"concept\":\"costs-in-r\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"The base rates\",\"text\":\"Grade A as documented facts: most retail traders lose, and day traders most of all. These are base rates to plan from, not a verdict on you.\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"bars\",\"bars\":[{\"label\":\"EU retail CFD accounts that lost: low end\",\"value\":74,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"EU retail CFD accounts that lost: high end\",\"value\":89,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"Brazil: persistent day traders who lost\",\"value\":97,\"role\":\"down\"}],\"unit\":\"%\",\"steps\":[\"ESMA, 2018: national analyses found 74% to 89% of retail CFD accounts lost money.\",\"Brazil: 97% of persistent day traders lost; 1.1% earned more than the minimum wage. Taiwan: under 1% reliably profit.\"]},\"concept\":\"retail-loss-rates\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Constraints to style\",\"prompt\":\"Match each constraint to the style or rule that fits it.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"A full-time job, free only in the evening\",\"right\":\"Swing or position trading, orders placed after work\"},{\"left\":\"Free mornings, living in Romania\",\"right\":\"Europe's 10:00 open, or the 15:00–19:00 overlap\"},{\"left\":\"A small account\",\"right\":\"Small sizes and fewer trades: swing\"},{\"left\":\"Low tolerance for drawdowns\",\"right\":\"Lower risk per trade\"},{\"left\":\"Impulsive under stress\",\"right\":\"Automatic orders and a longer horizon\"}],\"columns\":[\"Constraint\",\"Fits\"],\"concept\":\"trading-styles\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"The swing trader's risk\",\"prompt\":\"Swing long at 100.00, stop 98.00. Overnight news: the next open is 94.00, and the stop fills there. What's the result, in R, before costs?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"−1R\",\"feedback\":\"−1R is a loss at the stop. The stop filled at the open, 6.00 below entry, against a 2.00 risk.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"−2R\",\"feedback\":\"The loss is 6.00 a unit against 2.00 planned: three times.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"−3R\"}],\"answer\":\"c\"},\"reveal\":\"−3R: 6.00 lost a unit against a 2.00 risk. Swing trades pay fewer costs but carry overnight and weekend gaps. 2.9.8 sizes positions for them.\",\"concept\":\"gap-risk\"},{\"kind\":\"fields\",\"title\":\"Your trading window\",\"prompt\":\"Write down when you can actually trade, in your own time zone. A plan is only real if your days allow it.\",\"fields\":[{\"id\":\"hours\",\"label\":\"Hours you can watch the market on a weekday\",\"kind\":\"stepper\",\"min\":0,\"max\":12,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\" h\",\"default\":2},{\"id\":\"window\",\"label\":\"When, in your time zone\",\"kind\":\"text\",\"default\":\"\",\"placeholder\":\"e.g. 19:00–21:00, after work\",\"required\":true},{\"id\":\"style\",\"label\":\"Your style, and why\",\"kind\":\"text\",\"default\":\"\",\"placeholder\":\"e.g. Swing: I can't watch the US open from work\",\"required\":true}],\"correct\":\"Saved. Your trading window goes into your plan: no new entries outside it.\",\"concept\":\"trading-styles\"},{\"kind\":\"callout\",\"tone\":\"key\",\"text\":\"Deciding not to day trade is a valid result of this course. Choose the style your costs, hours and temperament can sustain, not the one that looks busiest.\"}],\"check\":[\"q-2.9.6-1\",\"q-2.9.6-2\",\"q-2.9.6-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"Day trading avoids overnight gaps but pays costs most often; swing and position trade costs for gap risk.\",\"Weekly costs in R = trades × cost ÷ stop: 10 × 0.20 ÷ 1.00 = 2.00R.\",\"Pick the style your hours, account and temper allow; not day trading is a valid choice.\"]}},\"2.9.7\":{\"unit\":\"2.9\",\"courseId\":\"2.9.7\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"A scalper on LABX near 100 targets 0.30 and stops at 0.30. Round-trip costs are about 0.20 per unit. What win rate breaks even?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"slider\",\"min\":50,\"max\":100,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"%\",\"start\":50,\"answer\":83,\"tolerance\":2,\"feedback\":[{\"from\":50,\"to\":60,\"text\":\"That would be true with no costs. Here a win nets only 0.10 and a loss costs 0.50.\"},{\"from\":61,\"to\":80,\"text\":\"Closer, but costs take more. Break-even = 0.50 ÷ (0.50 + 0.10).\"},{\"from\":86,\"to\":100,\"text\":\"A bit too high: 0.50 ÷ 0.60 = 83.3%.\"}]},\"compare\":\"You guessed {yours}. A win nets 0.10 and a loss costs 0.50: 0.50 ÷ 0.60 = 83.3%. A coin-flip setup would have to win five times in six.\",\"concept\":\"cost-drag\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Costs don't shrink with the target\",\"text\":\"[[scalping|Scalping]] aims for many small moves. The costs don't shrink with the target: on LABX a [[round-trip|round trip]] is about 0.20 per unit whether you aim for 0.30 or 3.00. The share of the gross profit that costs take is [[cost-drag|cost drag]]. The smaller the target, the bigger it gets.\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Break-even after costs\",\"lines\":[\"Target 0.50, stop 0.50, costs 0.20 per unit.\",\"A win nets 0.50 − 0.20 = 0.30. A loss costs 0.50 + 0.20 = 0.70.\",\"Break-even = loss ÷ (loss + win) = 0.70 ÷ 1.00 = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":70,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"%\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":50,\"text\":\"That's the break-even before costs. Use the loss and the win after costs: 0.70 and 0.30.\"},{\"value\":30,\"text\":\"That's the win's share. Break-even is the loss's share: 0.70 ÷ 1.00.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. 70%.\"},\"after\":\"With equal stop and target d, the formula becomes (d + 0.20) ÷ 2d. At 0.50 the trade must win 7 times in 10 just to break even.\",\"concept\":\"cost-drag\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"The cost wall\",\"text\":\"Break-even win rate when the stop and the target are both d, with 0.20 of costs per unit: (d + 0.20) ÷ 2d.\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"bars\",\"bars\":[{\"label\":\"d = 0.30\",\"value\":83.3,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"d = 0.50\",\"value\":70,\"role\":\"down\"},{\"label\":\"d = 1.00\",\"value\":60,\"role\":\"accent\"},{\"label\":\"d = 2.00\",\"value\":55,\"role\":\"accent\"},{\"label\":\"d = 4.00\",\"value\":52.5,\"role\":\"accent\"}],\"unit\":\"%\",\"steps\":[\"At 0.30 and 0.50, you must win 83% or 70% of the time.\",\"At 1.00, 60%: still a high bar for any setup.\",\"At 2.00 and 4.00, costs fade: 55% and 52.5%.\"]},\"concept\":\"cost-drag\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Cost drag\",\"lines\":[\"Cost drag = costs ÷ average gross profit per trade.\",\"LABX scalp: 0.20 ÷ 0.30 = 67% of each win goes to costs.\",\"FX swing: 1 pip of cost on a 150-pip target is under 1%.\",\"FX scalp: 1 pip of cost on a 5-pip target. Cost drag = ?\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":20,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"%\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":5,\"text\":\"That's 5 ÷ 1, upside down. Divide the cost by the target: 1 ÷ 5.\"},{\"value\":0.2,\"text\":\"Right ratio; type it as a percentage: 20.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. 20%: a fifth of every winning scalp.\"},\"after\":\"Same spread, two targets: 20% for the scalp, 0.7% for the swing. Only the target changed. Cost drag puts styles side by side on one number.\",\"concept\":\"cost-drag\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Who's on the other side\",\"text\":\"At the shortest horizons you trade against [[market-maker|market makers]] and high-frequency firms. They earn the spread instead of paying it, see the [[order-book|order book]] first and react in microseconds. The retail record is grim: in Brazil, 97% of persistent day traders lost. Professional market making is a different business, covered in Level 3.\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"The monthly bill\",\"prompt\":\"A scalper makes 25 trades a day, 20 trading days a month, and pays 0.10R in costs per trade. What do costs take in a month?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"5R\",\"feedback\":\"That's two days. Over a month: 25 × 20 × 0.10R.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"12R\",\"feedback\":\"12R is the bill at 6 trades a day. At 25 a day it's much larger.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"50R\"}],\"answer\":\"c\"},\"reveal\":\"25 × 20 × 0.10R = 50R a month. Even 6 trades a day cost 12R. Before any skill shows up, the edge has to pay that bill.\",\"concept\":\"cost-drag\"},{\"kind\":\"predict\",\"title\":\"Find the wall\",\"prompt\":\"Stop and target are equal, d, and costs are 0.20 per unit. What's the smallest d at which the break-even win rate falls to 60%?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"slider\",\"min\":0.3,\"max\":3,\"step\":0.1,\"start\":0.3,\"answer\":1,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"feedback\":[{\"from\":0.3,\"to\":0.95,\"text\":\"Too small. At 0.90 break-even is 1.10 ÷ 1.80 = 61.1%.\"},{\"from\":1.05,\"to\":3,\"text\":\"That works, but a smaller d does too: at 1.00, 1.20 ÷ 2.00 = 60%.\"}]},\"reveal\":\"d = 1.00: (1.00 + 0.20) ÷ 2.00 = 60%. Below that the wall rises fast: 70% at 0.50, 83% at 0.30.\",\"concept\":\"cost-drag\"}],\"check\":[\"q-2.9.7-1\",\"q-2.9.7-2\",\"q-2.9.7-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"With stop = target = 0.30 and 0.20 costs, break-even is 83%: (d + 0.20) ÷ 2d.\",\"Cost drag = costs ÷ average gross profit: 67% on a 0.30 scalp, under 1% on a 150-pip swing.\",\"Retail scalpers pay the spread to professionals who earn it; the base rates are grim.\"]}},\"2.9.8\":{\"unit\":\"2.9\",\"courseId\":\"2.9.8\",\"hook\":{\"prompt\":\"Friday: long 47 LABX at 100.00, stop 98.00, planned loss 98.66. Monday's first bid is 95.50. What do you lose?\",\"control\":{\"kind\":\"slider\",\"min\":0,\"max\":300,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"vUSD\",\"start\":99,\"answer\":216,\"tolerance\":5,\"feedback\":[{\"from\":0,\"to\":110,\"text\":\"The stop doesn't cap the loss in a gap. It fills at the first price, 95.50, 4.50 below entry.\"},{\"from\":111,\"to\":205,\"text\":\"More. 47 × 4.50 = 211.50 before the two commissions.\"},{\"from\":228,\"to\":300,\"text\":\"Less: 211.50 on price plus about 4.60 in commissions.\"}]},\"compare\":\"You guessed {yours}. The stop fills at 95.50: 47 × 4.50 = 211.50, plus commissions of 2.35 and 2.25: 216.10, 2.2 times the plan.\",\"concept\":\"weekend-and-holiday-risk\"},\"learn\":[{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"An order, not a guarantee\",\"text\":\"A [[stop-loss]] is an order, not a guarantee. While the market is shut, news keeps arriving. On Monday the first price can be far past your stop, and the stop fills there, at the first price available. That jump is a [[weekend-gap|weekend gap]]. Holidays do the same, and their thin markets widen [[spread|spreads]] too.\"},{\"kind\":\"figure\",\"title\":\"Friday's plan, Monday's open\",\"text\":\"Daily LABX candles. The position is opened on Friday and held over the weekend.\",\"figure\":{\"kind\":\"chart\",\"chart\":{\"candles\":[{\"o\":98.6,\"h\":99.4,\"l\":98.2,\"c\":99.1},{\"o\":99.1,\"h\":99.8,\"l\":98.7,\"c\":99.5},{\"o\":99.5,\"h\":100.3,\"l\":99.1,\"c\":99.9},{\"o\":99.9,\"h\":100.6,\"l\":99.5,\"c\":100.2},{\"o\":100.2,\"h\":100.7,\"l\":99.6,\"c\":100.05},{\"o\":95.55,\"h\":96.3,\"l\":95.1,\"c\":95.8},{\"o\":95.8,\"h\":96.6,\"l\":95.3,\"c\":96.2}],\"visible\":5},\"steps\":[{\"text\":\"Friday: long 47 at 100.00, stop at 98.00. The plan risks 98.66.\",\"visible\":5,\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":100,\"role\":\"entry\",\"label\":\"Entry\"},{\"kind\":\"level\",\"price\":98,\"role\":\"stop\",\"label\":\"Stop\"}]},{\"text\":\"Monday opens far below the stop. Nothing traded in between.\",\"visible\":7},{\"text\":\"The stop fills at the first bid, 95.50: 216.10 lost, 2.2 times the plan.\",\"layers\":[{\"kind\":\"marker\",\"index\":5,\"price\":95.5,\"label\":\"Filled 95.50\",\"role\":\"down\"}]}]},\"concept\":\"weekend-and-holiday-risk\"},{\"kind\":\"worked\",\"title\":\"Plan for the worst gap\",\"lines\":[\"This course plans weekend holds for a gap of up to 3 × the daily ATR.\",\"Daily ATR 2.00: a 6.00 gap, so a worst-case exit at 94.00.\",\"47 units, sized for a normal day, lose 47 × 6.00 = ? before costs.\"],\"ask\":{\"answer\":282,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\",\"feedback\":[{\"value\":94,\"text\":\"That's 47 × 2.00, the loss at the normal stop. The gap exit is 6.00 below entry.\"},{\"value\":211.5,\"text\":\"That's Monday's 95.50 fill. The worst case the plan uses is 94.00, 6.00 below entry.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. 282.00 before costs.\"},\"after\":\"With commissions, 286.56: nearly three times the planned 98.66. To keep a gap like that within 2R, 200.00, the size has to shrink. That's the Try.\",\"concept\":\"weekend-and-holiday-risk\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Holidays thin the market\",\"text\":\"Around US Thanksgiving, from Christmas to New Year, and through Europe's August, fewer traders are at their desks. Order books thin out, spreads widen and the same order moves price further: more [[slippage]]. One market can be shut while another trades, so prices jump when it reopens. Treat a long holiday like a long weekend.\"},{\"kind\":\"explain\",\"title\":\"Rollover\",\"text\":\"Open spot [[forex|FX]] positions roll to the next day at 17:00 New York time: the [[rollover]]. That's typically 00:00 in Bucharest, or 23:00 in the weeks when US and EU clocks disagree. Spreads often widen for some minutes around it, so a tight stop can be hit by the spread alone. Financing charges come in 2.14.4.\"},{\"kind\":\"match\",\"title\":\"Situation to policy\",\"prompt\":\"Match each situation to the policy this course uses.\",\"pairs\":[{\"left\":\"Holding a position over a weekend\",\"right\":\"Cut the size so a 3 × ATR gap stays within 2R\"},{\"left\":\"A market holiday ahead\",\"right\":\"Reduce the size, or skip the trade\"},{\"left\":\"The 15 minutes around FX rollover\",\"right\":\"No tight stops, no new entries\"},{\"left\":\"An ordinary weekday session\",\"right\":\"Normal size, normal stop\"}],\"columns\":[\"Situation\",\"Policy\"],\"concept\":\"weekend-and-holiday-risk\"}],\"task\":{\"widget\":\"position-size\",\"goal\":\"Weekend hold: entry 100.00, daily ATR 2.00, worst-case gap 3 × ATR. Set the stop at the gap exit, then type the most units that lose at most 200.00 (2R) there.\",\"start\":{\"account\":10000,\"riskPct\":2,\"entry\":100,\"stop\":98,\"units\":47},\"locked\":[\"account\",\"riskPct\",\"entry\"],\"hidden\":[\"simpleQty\",\"qty\"],\"success\":{\"all\":[{\"field\":\"stop\",\"eq\":94},{\"field\":\"units\",\"eq\":32}]},\"feedback\":[{\"when\":{\"field\":\"stop\",\"gt\":94},\"text\":\"{stop:2} is a normal-day stop. The worst case is 3 × 2.00 = 6.00 below entry: 94.00.\"},{\"when\":{\"field\":\"stop\",\"lt\":94},\"text\":\"That's past the worst case the plan uses: 100.00 − 6.00 = 94.00.\"},{\"when\":{\"field\":\"units\",\"eq\":33},\"text\":\"33 units lose 201.21 at 94.00 with both commissions, just over 200.00.\"},{\"when\":{\"field\":\"units\",\"gt\":33},\"text\":\"{units} units would lose {lossAtStop:2} in that gap, over 200.00.\"},{\"when\":{\"field\":\"units\",\"lt\":32},\"text\":\"{units} units fit, losing {lossAtStop:2}. One more would still fit: find the most that does.\"}],\"correct\":\"Right. 32 units lose 195.11 if Monday opens at 94.00, within 2R. On a normal day you'd hold 47. Your real stop stays at 98.00; it just can't be trusted over a weekend.\",\"hints\":[\"The gap exit is 100.00 − 3 × 2.00 = 94.00.\",\"Each unit loses 6.00 there, plus about 0.10 in commissions.\",\"200.00 ÷ 6.097 ≈ 32.8. Round down.\"],\"showMe\":{\"stop\":94,\"units\":32},\"concept\":\"weekend-and-holiday-risk\"},\"check\":[\"q-2.9.8-1\",\"q-2.9.8-2\",\"q-2.9.8-3\"],\"recap\":{\"takeaways\":[\"A stop fills at the first price after a gap: 47 units lost 216.10, not 98.66.\",\"Size weekend holds for a 3 × ATR gap within 2R: 32 units instead of 47.\",\"FX rolls over at 17:00 New York, usually 00:00 in Bucharest; spreads widen around it.\"]}}}");
